From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: rebuild the WFDMA rings after PATCH_FINISH_REQ

Reading the endpoint's config space live shows the whole story: PCI_COMMAND
is already 0x0000 at the first sample after 0x07 is fetched, so the chip
clears its own PCIe configuration the moment it accepts the patch.  The BARs
come back by hand, and then LPCTL reads 0x00000004 - BIT(2), FW_OWN_STAT, the
same value the vendor firmware leaves behind - so the MCU is alive and the
patch is running.  What stays dead is the WFDMA: the hardware write index is
back at zero while the software head/tail are still at 4/3, so the chip waits
for descriptor zero and never looks at the semaphore release that was just
queued.  That is the 20 s timeout, not a watchdog.

Rebuild the rings instead of giving up: drop the software indices to zero,
rewrite base/count/cidx/didx through the normal queue init path, and let the
chip start fetching again from a state both sides agree on.  Only the
diagnostics call this, buffers keep their DMA addresses, and the 0x07 path
still returns fire-and-forget.

With the rings back the semaphore release becomes the first command that has
to be answered, so the next boot either shows sem=3 and a firmware download
that continues into RAM code, or it shows the release timing out on a channel
that is genuinely dead.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c
@@ -1038,6 +1038,51 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/**
+ * mtk_pci_dma_diag_resync - rebuild the WFDMA rings after PATCH_FINISH_REQ
+ *
+ * The ROM clears its own PCIe configuration the moment it accepts 0x07, and
+ * the WFDMA goes back to index zero together with it.  The BARs can be put
+ * back by hand from the host, but the rings stay dead: the hardware reads
+ * descriptor zero next while the software pointers are sitting in the middle
+ * of the ring, so nothing is ever fetched again and every following command
+ * times out on a chip that is still running (LPCTL reports FW_OWN_STAT).
+ *
+ * Put the software indices back to zero and rewrite base/count/cidx/didx so
+ * both sides agree on where the ring starts.  Buffers are deliberately not
+ * remapped - the descriptors still carry valid DMA addresses and this path is
+ * only reachable from the bring-up diagnostics.
+ */
+void mtk_pci_dma_diag_resync(struct mtk_bus_trans *btrans)
+{
+	struct pci_trans *trans = to_pci_trans(btrans);
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < trans->txq_num; i++) {
+		if (trans->txq[i].q.disable)
+			continue;
+
+		trans->txq[i].q.head = 0;
+		trans->txq[i].q.tail = 0;
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < trans->rxq_num; i++) {
+		if (trans->rxq[i].q.disable)
+			continue;
+
+		trans->rxq[i].q.head = 0;
+		trans->rxq[i].q.tail = 0;
+	}
+
+	pci_chip_dma_init(trans);
+	pci_dma_hw_init_queues(trans);
+	pci_chip_dma_enable(trans);
+
+	dev_err(to_device(trans),
+		"diag: WFDMA resynced, %d txq / %d rxq back to index 0\n",
+		trans->txq_num, trans->rxq_num);
+}
+
 static void
 pci_dma_hw_exit_queues(struct pci_trans *trans)
 {
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
@@ -231,6 +231,9 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* bus layer helper, only used by the PATCH_FINISH_REQ diagnostics */
+void mtk_pci_dma_diag_resync(struct mtk_bus_trans *btrans);
+
 /*
  * PATCH_FINISH_REQ carries a check_crc flag.  With it set the ROM verifies
  * what it received against the CRC in the patch header, so a rejection
@@ -318,6 +321,13 @@
 		(unsigned long long)bar0,
 		bus_read(dev->bus_trans, DIAG48_LPCTL_ADDR),
 		bus_read(dev->bus_trans, DIAG48_ON_MISC_ADDR));
+
+	/*
+	 * The BARs alone are not enough: the WFDMA came back at index zero
+	 * while our head/tail are still pointing into the middle of the ring,
+	 * so the chip never sees another descriptor.  Rebuild the rings.
+	 */
+	mtk_pci_dma_diag_resync(dev->bus_trans);
 }
 
 static int
